window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 1","Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome","Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction","Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology","Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 1: the four topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: myocarditis, pericarditis and cardiac tamponade (Part 1). Topic 2: acute coronary syndrome …"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"part-1-myocarditis-pericarditis-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","Topic 1, the first lecture of NUR 406: myocarditis, then the pericardium, how inflammation leads to pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, the bedside signs, pulsus paradoxus, and treatment.","Myocarditis","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used.","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood.","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","Pulsus Paradoxus","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added.","Pericarditis on the ECG","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","Treating Pericarditis","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","a":"part-2a-acute-coronary-syndrome","k":["Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","Topic 2, first half: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, and how ACS differs from stable angina.","What ACS Is","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible.","The ACS Spectrum","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section).","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for.","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on.","Why Plaque Ruptures","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area.","Unstable Angina","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER.","Stable Angina vs ACS","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"part-2b-myocardial-infarction","k":["Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","Topic 2, second half: the signs of MI, what an acute MI is, what happens to heart tissue over time, the zones of damage, STEMI vs NSTEMI, and the LAD.","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest.","Acute Myocardial Infarction","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible.","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes.","Zones of Damage","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","a":"part-3-acute-mi-pharmacology","k":["Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","Topic 3, the second part of the same ACS PowerPoint: the initial drugs, fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, nitroglycerin safety, reperfusion injury, and the key takeaways. Diagnostics and surgical reperfusion are covered in med-surg, not …","Initial Drug Treatment","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size).","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated.","Nitroglycerin Safety","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it.","Reperfusion Injury","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation.","Key Takeaways","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"part-4-aneurysms-aortic-dissection","k":["Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","Topic 4: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, the types by location and shape, assessing a AAA, and the two big complications, rupture and aortic dissection.","What an Aneurysm Is","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component","Types of Aneurysms","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil.","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\"","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death.","Managing Aortic Dissection","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1. Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B) and can disrupt conduction. Normal pericardial fluid is 15–50 mL; about 200 mL compresses the heart into cardiac tamponade, a medical emergen…","2A. Acute Coronary Syndrome — ACS = unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI; time is tissue. Prinzmetal's is vasospasm, often at rest, treated with nitrates. Unstable plaque: big lipid core, thin fibrous cap, inflammation (CRP). First-time chest pai…","2B. Myocardial Infarction — MI is irreversible ischemic necrosis; ECG changes stay and troponin trends up. Myocardial cells can't store ATP: contraction drops in 1–2 minutes, irreversible injury from 30 minutes to 4 hours, so reperfuse with…","3. Acute MI Pharmacology — Take vital signs before every drug. Aspirin: chew the first dose; decreases mortality in STEMI. Morphine: watch respiratory depression and hypotension; may inhibit antiplatelets. Nitroglycerin: reduces preload and…","4. Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Primarily from hypertension; turbulent flow, a bruit, rupture risk. Saccular (one side, wide neck), berry (small neck, circle of Willis; drain, clip or coil), fusiform (whole circumference, monitored), fal…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericardial-disease.html","t":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","secs":[{"s":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Myocarditis","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","Pulsus Paradoxus","Pericarditis on the ECG","Treating Pericarditis","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first lecture of NUR 406, a short voiceover on pericardial disease. It covers myocarditis (inflammation of the heart muscle), then the pericardium itself: how inflammation of the pericardium leads to fluid buildup, pericardial e…"]},{"s":"Myocarditis▾","a":"myocarditis","k":["Myocarditis▾","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used."]},{"s":"The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","a":"the-pericardium-pericardial-fluid","k":["The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood."]},{"s":"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"acute-pericarditis-vs-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade."]},{"s":"Pulsus Paradoxus▾","a":"pulsus-paradoxus","k":["Pulsus Paradoxus▾","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added."]},{"s":"Pericarditis on the ECG▾","a":"pericarditis-on-the-ecg","k":["Pericarditis on the ECG▾","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","ST elevation in MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction. Compare the two if needed"]},{"s":"Treating Pericarditis▾","a":"treating-pericarditis","k":["Treating Pericarditis▾","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly.","Aspirin for acute MI is a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acs-mi.html","t":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","secs":[{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","What ACS Is","The ACS Spectrum","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Why Plaque Ruptures","Unstable Angina","Stable Angina vs ACS","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","Acute Myocardial Infarction","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Zones of Damage","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the ACS voiceover: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, how ACS differs from stable angina, the signs of MI, what happens to heart ti…"]},{"s":"What ACS Is▾","a":"what-acs-is","k":["What ACS Is▾","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible."]},{"s":"The ACS Spectrum▾","a":"the-acs-spectrum","k":["The ACS Spectrum▾","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section)."]},{"s":"Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","a":"prinzmetal-s-variant-or-vasospastic-angina","k":["Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for."]},{"s":"What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","a":"what-makes-plaque-unstable","k":["What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on."]},{"s":"Why Plaque Ruptures▾","a":"why-plaque-ruptures","k":["Why Plaque Ruptures▾","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area."]},{"s":"Unstable Angina▾","a":"unstable-angina","k":["Unstable Angina▾","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER."]},{"s":"Stable Angina vs ACS▾","a":"stable-angina-vs-acs","k":["Stable Angina vs ACS▾","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"signs-symptoms-of-myocardial-infarction","k":["Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest."]},{"s":"Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"acute-myocardial-infarction","k":["Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible."]},{"s":"Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","a":"ischemia-infarction-atp","k":["Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes."]},{"s":"Zones of Damage▾","a":"zones-of-damage","k":["Zones of Damage▾","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…"]},{"s":"STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","a":"stemi-vs-nstemi","k":["STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","Pericarditis can also show ST elevation and can look like an MI. See Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade"]},{"s":"The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","a":"the-lad-the-widow-maker","k":["The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acute-mi-pharm.html","t":"Acute MI Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Acute MI Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Initial Drug Treatment","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","Nitroglycerin Safety","Reperfusion Injury","Key Takeaways","Self-Test Flashcards","The second, shorter part of the ACS voiceover. It covers the initial drug treatment of an acute MI (oxygen, morphine, aspirin, nitroglycerin, beta blockers and thrombolytics), fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, reperfusion injury, and the…","The pathophysiology of ACS and MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction"]},{"s":"Initial Drug Treatment▾","a":"initial-drug-treatment","k":["Initial Drug Treatment▾","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size)."]},{"s":"Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","a":"fibrinolytic-therapy-alteplase-tpa","k":["Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin Safety▾","a":"nitroglycerin-safety","k":["Nitroglycerin Safety▾","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it."]},{"s":"Reperfusion Injury▾","a":"reperfusion-injury","k":["Reperfusion Injury▾","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation."]},{"s":"Key Takeaways▾","a":"key-takeaways","k":["Key Takeaways▾","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-aneurysms.html","t":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","secs":[{"s":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","What an Aneurysm Is","Types of Aneurysms","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Managing Aortic Dissection","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the aneurysm voiceover: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, risk factors, the types by location and by shape (saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms), assessing an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and the two big compli…"]},{"s":"What an Aneurysm Is▾","a":"what-an-aneurysm-is","k":["What an Aneurysm Is▾","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component"]},{"s":"Types of Aneurysms▾","a":"types-of-aneurysms","k":["Types of Aneurysms▾","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil."]},{"s":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","a":"abdominal-aortic-aneurysm-aaa","k":["Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …"]},{"s":"Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"complications-rupture-aortic-dissection","k":["Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\""]},{"s":"Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","a":"aneurysm-vs-aortic-dissection","k":["Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death."]},{"s":"Managing Aortic Dissection▾","a":"managing-aortic-dissection","k":["Managing Aortic Dissection▾","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment.","Beta blockers and nitroglycerin in acute MI are a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 1's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week …"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-four-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","Four pages are easier to hold in your head when you see what each lecture is really about. Topics 2 and 3 are the two halves of one PowerPoint.","The Heart Muscle and Its Sac: Inflammation — Topic 1, Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (all of it) — The first lecture of 406. Inflammation of the myocardium (myocarditis, usually viral) can range from mild to heart failure and…","The Coronary Arteries: Blockage — Topic 2, Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction (the ACS sections first, then the MI sections) — The longest lecture. Blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised, and the si…","Treating the Blockage — Topic 3, Acute MI Pharmacology (all of it) — The second, shorter half of the ACS PowerPoint. Each drug has a job: oxygen raises delivery to the ischemic muscle, morphine eases pain, aspirin keeps the thrombus from gr…","The Artery Wall: Weakening and Tearing — Topic 4, Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection (all of it) — Back out to the vessels. Uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall until it bulges (an aneurysm), and a weakened wall can tear (dissecti…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order, and read Topic 2 before Topic 3: the pharmacology lecture assumes the plaque, clot and \"time is tissue\" ideas from ACS."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B), treated with antivirals and anti-inflammatories depending on severity. Pericarditis brings sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breathin…","2 — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — ACS is unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI, and time is tissue. Unstable plaque (big lipid core, thin cap, inflammation) ruptures under sympathetic stress and a thrombus forms. Unstable an…","3 — Acute MI Pharmacology — Oxygen, morphine (now used more cautiously), chewed aspirin, nitroglycerin and beta blockers, all after checking vital signs. Aspirin decreases mortality; nitroglycerin does not. PCI is first choice for STEMI; al…","4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Aneurysms come primarily from hypertension and atherosclerosis, cause turbulent flow and a bruit, and are most common in the aorta and cerebral arteries. Saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms diff…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","ST elevation on the ECG — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"STEMI vs NSTEMI\" (elevation in STEMI; normal or slight depression in NSTEMI) — The same page's \"Prinzmetal's Angina\" section (it may show ST elevation, which return…","Aspirin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (chew the first dose; suppresses platelet aggregation; decreases mortality in STEMI) — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Treating Pericarditis,\" where aspirin may be se…","Nitrates and nitroglycerin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" and \"Nitroglycerin Safety\" (reduces preload and afterload; no sildenafil) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: nitrates relax the spasm in Prinzmetal…","Beta blockers — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (lower heart rate and contractility, so lower oxygen demand) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"Managing Aortic Dissection,\" to decrease heart rate and blood pressure and preven…","Blood pressure and force on the vessel wall — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"What an Aneurysm Is\" (uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"Why Plaque Ruptures\" (sympathetic s…","Atherosclerosis — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"The ACS Spectrum\" and \"What Makes Plaque Unstable\" — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a primary driver in \"Risk Factors,\" with fusiform aneurysms related to diffuse sclerotic…","Inflammation — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (inflammation of the myocardium and of the pericardium) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstable,\" where inflammation (measured by CRP) makes…","Conduction problems and dysrhythmias — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (inflammation disrupts conduction) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: Prinzmetal's can cause serious dysrhythmias, and scar …","Heart failure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (mild all the way up to heart failure) — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Reperfusion Injury,\" where stunned myocardium can cause heart failure.","Poor perfusion and falling blood pressure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade\" (decreased cardiac output: hypotension, confusion, dizziness, JVD) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial I…","Bleeding — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Fibrinolytic Therapy\" (bleeding is the main adverse effect; no thrombolytic after a recent hemorrhagic stroke) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly; late dissection bleed…","Dye studies and angiography — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: saccular aneurysms on an angiogram, berry aneurysms diagnosed by angiography, dissection by CT or MRA — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstabl…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the four pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any …","Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency: the fluid has to be removed quickly (pericardiocentesis), because the chambers can't stretch and fill. Watch for decreased cardiac output: hypotension…","Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — First-time chest pain is unstable angina until proven otherwise. Unstable angina is a medical emergency: call 911, never drive to the ER. Take an impending sense of doom very seriously. Time…","Acute MI Pharmacology — Take heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before giving any of these drugs. Morphine: respiratory depression and hypotension. Nitroglycerin: never with sildenafil (profound hypotension); IV only if blood…","Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — No deep palpation of a suspected AAA (rupture risk); light palpation only. A ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly. Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency where minutes matter; without intervention it progr…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"The emergency is when blood can't get where it needs to go.\" Tamponade stops the heart from filling, a complete clot in a STEMI stops blood reaching the heart muscle, and a rupture or dissection lets blood escape the vessel. The lectures c…","\"The quality of the pain is a clue.\" Pericarditis: sharp and worse with deep breathing. ACS: longer, more severe, an elephant on the chest, no relief with nitroglycerin. Aortic dissection: sudden, severe and tearing. And some don't hurt at …","\"Look-alikes on the ECG.\" Pericarditis can show ST elevation like an MI; Prinzmetal's angina can show ST elevation that returns to normal; unstable angina's ECG changes return to normal while an MI's stay. The ECG alone isn't the whole stor…","\"Check before you give.\" The MI drugs can push an unstable patient over the edge, so vital signs come first, and several drugs carry a condition: morphine if they can tolerate it, IV nitroglycerin if blood pressure tolerates it, beta blocke…","\"Don't let the patient wait.\" Hesitating or delaying care is a big reason for MI deaths, while treated patients survive about 90 to 95% of the time. Teach patients to call 911 for unstable angina and to seek care if they suspect an MI: bett…"]}]},{"f":"week2-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 2","Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids","Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH","Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders","Part 2B — ABG Interpretation","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 2: the two topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: acid-base balance (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: ABGs and ABG interpretation (Parts 2A–2B). It inte…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","a":"part-1a-ph-hydrogen-acids","k":["Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","Topic 1, first voiceover (Acid base 1): what pH is, why hydrogen matters, normal serum pH and how pH varies in the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs non-volatile acids.","What pH Is","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14","Why Hydrogen Matters","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges.","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6","What pH Affects","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic.","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible.","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","a":"part-1b-how-the-body-defends-its-ph","k":["Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","Topic 1, second voiceover (Acid base 2): the three lines of defense, the buffer systems, the carbonic acid equation, cellular compensation and potassium, respiratory and renal control, and compensation.","The Three Lines of Defense","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term.","Buffer Systems","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…","The Carbonic Acid Equation","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU.","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution.","Respiratory Control of pH","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls.","Renal Control of pH","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it.","Compensation","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","What an ABG Measures","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture (Part 2A)."]},{"s":"Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","a":"part-2a-abgs-the-four-acid-base-disorders","k":["Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","Topic 2, first voiceover (ABGs): why ABGs are drawn, the normal values, the key terms, ROME, and each of the four disorders with its ABG pattern, causes, signs and treatment.","Why We Draw ABGs","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…","Normal ABG Values","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…","Key Terms","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change.","Metabolic Alkalosis","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","Respiratory Alkalosis","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings.","Respiratory Acidosis","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume.","Metabolic Acidosis","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","a":"part-2b-abg-interpretation","k":["Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","Topic 2, second voiceover (ABG Interpretation): how compensation works, the four levels of compensation, the step-by-step method, every worked example, and the two shortcut methods.","How Compensation Works","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed.","The Four Levels of Compensation","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG.","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating.","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript.","Two Shortcut Methods","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. pH, Hydrogen & Acids — More hydrogen ions = lower pH; the scale is logarithmic (each number is 10 times). Normal serum pH 7.35–7.45; below 7.35 is acidosis, above 7.45 alkalosis; below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life. Acidic …","1B. How the Body Defends Its pH — Buffers act in seconds, the lungs in minutes to hours, the kidneys in hours to days. Bicarbonate–carbonic acid is the main extracellular buffer; phosphate and protein buffers are intracellular. Know CO2 + H…","2A. ABGs & the Four Disorders — Know the numbers: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45, HCO3 24–29 (BMP CO2 = bicarb). ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics, antacids (treat with H2 bloc…","2B. ABG Interpretation — Step 1 pH (7.40 is the midpoint), step 2 PaCO2, step 3 HCO3, step 4 compensation. The value that matches the pH is the problem; the other one moving the same direction is compensating. Uncompensated: one abnormal va…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the four parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week2-acid-base-balance.html","t":"Acid-Base Balance","secs":[{"s":"Acid-Base Balance","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 1","What pH Is","Why Hydrogen Matters","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","What pH Affects","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","The Three Lines of Defense","Buffer Systems","The Carbonic Acid Equation","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Respiratory Control of pH","Renal Control of pH","Compensation","What an ABG Measures","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the two acid-base voiceovers. Part 1 covers what pH is, why hydrogen matters, the normal serum range and how pH varies around the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs n…"]},{"s":"What pH Is▾","a":"what-ph-is","k":["What pH Is▾","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14"]},{"s":"Why Hydrogen Matters▾","a":"why-hydrogen-matters","k":["Why Hydrogen Matters▾","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges."]},{"s":"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","a":"normal-ph-how-it-varies-in-the-body","k":["Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6"]},{"s":"What pH Affects▾","a":"what-ph-affects","k":["What pH Affects▾","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic."]},{"s":"Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","a":"acidosis-alkalosis-survivable-limits","k":["Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible."]},{"s":"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","a":"volatile-vs-non-volatile-acids","k":["Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"The Three Lines of Defense▾","a":"the-three-lines-of-defense","k":["The Three Lines of Defense▾","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term."]},{"s":"Buffer Systems▾","a":"buffer-systems","k":["Buffer Systems▾","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…"]},{"s":"The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","a":"the-carbonic-acid-equation","k":["The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU."]},{"s":"Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","a":"cellular-compensation-potassium","k":["Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution."]},{"s":"Respiratory Control of pH▾","a":"respiratory-control-of-ph","k":["Respiratory Control of pH▾","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls."]},{"s":"Renal Control of pH▾","a":"renal-control-of-ph","k":["Renal Control of pH▾","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it."]},{"s":"Compensation▾","a":"compensation","k":["Compensation▾","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","The four levels of compensation (adding \"corrected\") are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"What an ABG Measures▾","a":"what-an-abg-measures","k":["What an ABG Measures▾","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture.","Normal ABG values are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-abg-interpretation.html","t":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","secs":[{"s":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 2","Why We Draw ABGs","Normal ABG Values","Key Terms","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","Metabolic Alkalosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Acidosis","How Compensation Works","The Four Levels of Compensation","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Two Shortcut Methods","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the ABG voiceover and the ABG interpretation voiceover. It covers why we draw ABGs, the normal values, the key terms (primary event, primary disorder, compensation), the four acid-base disorders with their ABG patterns, c…","pH, buffers, the carbonic acid equation, and how the lungs and kidneys compensate are covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Why We Draw ABGs▾","a":"why-we-draw-abgs","k":["Why We Draw ABGs▾","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…"]},{"s":"Normal ABG Values▾","a":"normal-abg-values","k":["Normal ABG Values▾","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…"]},{"s":"Key Terms▾","a":"key-terms","k":["Key Terms▾","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis."]},{"s":"ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","a":"rome-the-four-primary-disorders","k":["ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change."]},{"s":"Metabolic Alkalosis▾","a":"metabolic-alkalosis","k":["Metabolic Alkalosis▾","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","The hydrogen–potassium shift is covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Respiratory Alkalosis▾","a":"respiratory-alkalosis","k":["Respiratory Alkalosis▾","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings."]},{"s":"Respiratory Acidosis▾","a":"respiratory-acidosis","k":["Respiratory Acidosis▾","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume."]},{"s":"Metabolic Acidosis▾","a":"metabolic-acidosis","k":["Metabolic Acidosis▾","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"How Compensation Works▾","a":"how-compensation-works","k":["How Compensation Works▾","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed."]},{"s":"The Four Levels of Compensation▾","a":"the-four-levels-of-compensation","k":["The Four Levels of Compensation▾","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG."]},{"s":"Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","a":"interpreting-an-abg-step-by-step","k":["Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating."]},{"s":"Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","a":"worked-examples-from-the-lecture","k":["Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript."]},{"s":"Two Shortcut Methods▾","a":"two-shortcut-methods","k":["Two Shortcut Methods▾","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 2","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics","The Two Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 2, so you can see how the two topics fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week toge…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-two-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","Each topic page holds two voiceovers, and each voiceover builds directly on the one before it.","The Scale and the Acids — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (the sections from \"What pH Is\" through \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\") — pH is a measure of hydrogen ions, inversely and logarithmically. The serum has to stay between 7.35 and 7.45, b…","The Defenses — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (from \"The Three Lines of Defense\" to the end) — Three lines of defense on three timescales: buffers in seconds, lungs in minutes to hours, kidneys in hours to days. The carbonic acid equation ties …","Reading the Gas — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"Why We Draw ABGs\" through \"Metabolic Acidosis\") — The ABG measures the equation's two ends: PaCO2 is the respiratory side and HCO3 is the metabolic side. That gives four disorders,…","Putting It Together — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"How Compensation Works\" to the end) — A four-step method (pH, PaCO2, HCO3, compensation), four levels of compensation, worked examples, and two shortcuts. Everything from the f…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. The interpretation steps only make sense once ROME and the carbonic acid equation do."]},{"s":"The Two Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-two-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Two Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Acid-Base Balance — More hydrogen ions mean a lower pH, and each step on the scale is tenfold. Serum pH must stay at 7.35–7.45 (below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life); acidic blood releases oxygen to tissues, alkalotic blood …","2 — ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Know pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45 and HCO3 24–29. ROME sorts the four disorders: respiratory acidosis (hypoventilation, overdose, COPD), respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation, panic, high vent settings), metab…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","The carbonic acid equation — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (know it backwards and forwards) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: it is why PaCO2 is the respiratory value and HCO3 the metabolic value, and why ROME works.","Compensation — Acid-Base Balance: \"Compensation\" (uncompensated, partial, complete; metabolic problems are compensated by the lungs, respiratory problems by the kidneys) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"How Compensation Works\" and \"The Four Le…","Speed of each defense — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Three Lines of Defense\" (seconds, minutes to hours, hours to days) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: respiratory alkalosis shows a normal bicarb because the kidneys can't respond acutely; acute res…","The COPD patient — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" and \"Renal Control of pH\" (retained CO2 pushes bicarb up; the kidneys hold on to bicarb) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: chronic respiratory acidosis, the acute-on-chronic exam…","Potassium shifts — Acid-Base Balance: \"Cellular Compensation & Potassium\" (in acidosis, hydrogen moves in and potassium moves out) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: hyperkalemia in metabolic acidosis; hypokalemia and cardiac issues in metabolic …","DKA and ketones — Acid-Base Balance: beta-hydroxybutyric acid as the DKA diagnostic, and the potassium shift to be revisited with DKA — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Metabolic Acidosis\" (DKA as a cause, Kussmaul respirations, insulin to lower…","Kidney failure and daily bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Renal Control of pH\" (renal failure can't maintain pH; patients take bicarb daily) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: renal failure as a cause of metabolic acidosis, and dialysis or bicarb whe…","Narcotics and respiratory depression — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (a low respiratory rate means CO2 can't be removed) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Respiratory Acidosis\" (overdose, too much narcotic or sedative, anesthe…","The ventilator — Acid-Base Balance: \"Respiratory Control of pH\" (ventilator settings can be adjusted to affect pH) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: a rate or tidal volume set too high causes respiratory alkalosis; raising them treats respirator…","Stomach acid and gut bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body\" (hydrochloric acid in the stomach) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: vomiting and NG suction remove that acid (metabolic alkalosis, treated with H2 blockers…","Lactic acid — Acid-Base Balance: \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\" (anaerobic byproduct; the lungs can't remove it) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: lactic acidosis, shock and cardiac arrest as causes of metabolic acidosis.","Hemoglobin — Acid-Base Balance: \"What pH Affects\" (oxygen release shifts with pH) — The same page's \"Buffer Systems\": hemoglobin as a protein buffer that carries CO2 as carbaminohemoglobin."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and emphasized statements from both pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Acid-Base Balance — A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life; outside 7.35–7.45, get back to normal as quickly as possible. Avoid alkalosis in an ill patient (hemoglobin holds on to oxygen). Acidosis can cause hyperkalemia and …","ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Use an ABG, not a VBG, when treatment changes depend on the result in a critically ill patient. Metabolic alkalosis can cause hypokalemia and cardiac issues. Ventilator settings can cause respiratory alkalosis. R…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Fast fixes are short; slow fixes last.\" Buffers act in seconds but only make small, temporary changes; cellular compensation is temporary; sodium bicarb in the ICU is a quick fix. The lungs act in minutes, but can't compensate for long. Th…","\"The organ that isn't the problem does the compensating.\" A lung problem is compensated by the kidneys (bicarb moves); a metabolic problem is compensated by the lungs (CO2 moves). That's why, in every disorder, the compensating value moves …","\"Breathing is both the cause and the cure.\" Hypoventilation causes respiratory acidosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic alkalosis; hyperventilation causes respiratory alkalosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic aci…","\"Potassium follows hydrogen in the opposite direction.\" Acidosis drives hydrogen into cells and potassium out (hyperkalemia); alkalosis pulls hydrogen out and potassium in (hypokalemia). Both can affect the heart.","\"Chronic looks calmer than acute.\" A chronic problem gives the kidneys time, so the pH drifts back toward normal (the compensated COPD patient). An acute problem doesn't, so the pH swings (the respiratory arrest, the panic attack, the COPD …"]}]}];
